SCIM -- Smart Common Input Method
=================================

SCIM is an input method framework to help you type languages other than English.
The input methods themselves come as separate packages:

  scim-tables        many languages https://github.com/scim-im/scim-tables
  scim-m17n          many languages https://github.com/scim-im/scim-m17n
  scim-uim           many languages https://github.com/scim-im/scim-uim
  scim-pinyin        Chinese        https://github.com/scim-im/scim-pinyin
  scim-novel-pinyin  Chinese        https://github.com/scim-im/scim-novel-pinyin
  scim-ccinput       Chinese        https://github.com/scim-im/scim-ccinput
  scim-chewing       Chinese        https://github.com/chewing/scim-chewing
  scim-array         Chinese        https://github.com/tzhuan/scim-array
  scim-anthy         Japanese       https://github.com/scim-im/scim-anthy
  scim-wnn           Japanese       https://github.com/scim-im/scim-wnn
  scim-skk           Japanese       https://github.com/tzhuan/scim-skk
  scim-prime         Japanese       https://github.com/tzhuan/scim-prime
  scim-canna         Japanese       https://github.com/tzhuan/scim-canna
  scim-hangul        Korean         https://github.com/tzhuan/scim-hangul
  scim-sinhala       Sinhala        https://github.com/tzhuan/scim-sinhala
  scim-unikey        Vietnamese     https://github.com/scim-im/scim-unikey

SCIM alone ships only rawcode, which enters a character by its code (hex
Unicode, or the current encoding's bytes).


Contents
========

  1. Install
  2. Set up your desktop
       2.1  GNOME, or any desktop where you want IBus
       2.2  Everything else
  3. Using it
  4. Packaging notes


1. Install
==========

  ./configure && make && sudo make install

From a git checkout, run ./bootstrap first.

Frontends, IM modules and GUI parts are built when their development packages
are present and skipped otherwise, so check the summary ./configure prints if
something you expected is missing.  To leave a part out deliberately, pass
--disable-NAME for any of:

  frontend-x11  frontend-wayland  frontend-ibus  frontend-socket
  gtk2-immodule  gtk3-immodule  gtk4-immodule  qt5-immodule  qt6-immodule
  panel-gtk  setup-ui  sni  kimpanel  filter-hanconv

Then log out and back in.  On a full desktop an autostart entry starts SCIM for
you; pointing your desktop at it is section 2.


2. Set up your desktop
======================

2.1  GNOME, or any desktop where you want IBus
----------------------------------------------

On GNOME/Wayland, IBus is the only way in -- not optional -- and SCIM registers
with it as an engine.  Follow this section too if you prefer IBus on another
desktop.

Give IBus the list of engines SCIM offers.  System-wide, as root:

  scim -f ibus -- --xml > /usr/share/ibus/component/scim.xml

That lists the engines installed system-wide.  If an engine also supports user
data -- scim-tables, for example, reads tables from your home directory -- a
manifest generated as root cannot see those, and only a personal manifest lets
IBus scan your own engines.

For that, IBUS_COMPONENT_PATH has to include your own component directory, and
it has to be set before ibus starts.  It replaces IBus's default search path
instead of adding to it, so list the system directory as well, or every other
input method on the system disappears:

  IBUS_COMPONENT_PATH=/home/your-user-name/.local/share/ibus/component:/usr/share/ibus/component

Then generate the manifest as yourself rather than as root, into whichever
directory you listed:

  mkdir -p ~/.local/share/ibus/component
  scim -f ibus -- --xml > ~/.local/share/ibus/component/scim.xml

Any directory you can read will do -- ~/.local/share/ibus/component is only the
conventional choice, and IBus scans exactly what IBUS_COMPONENT_PATH names.

On a systemd system, ibus runs as a user service and reads
~/.config/environment.d, so the variable goes there:

  mkdir -p ~/.config/environment.d
  echo 'IBUS_COMPONENT_PATH=${HOME}/.local/share/ibus/component:/usr/share/ibus/component' \
      > ~/.config/environment.d/scim-ibus.conf

On other systems, set it wherever your session's environment is defined; all
that matters is that ibus inherits it.

Log out and back in, then add SCIM's engines under Settings -> Keyboard -> Input
Sources.

Whenever an engine is installed or removed, regenerate the manifests you use --
the system one, the personal one, or both -- so they reflect what is actually
there, then log out and back in again.  Merely enabling or disabling an engine
needs nothing: GNOME and SCIM keep that in step by themselves.

Do not set GTK_IM_MODULE or QT_IM_MODULE to scim here: that takes applications
off IBus and breaks it.  Install IBus's own GTK2, GTK3 and GTK4 IM modules if
you use GTK applications under XWayland, or any GTK2 application; your
distribution may package them separately from ibus itself.  Qt needs nothing
extra.

2.2  Everything else
--------------------

On Wayland it works as installed: no variables, no IM modules.  On KDE Plasma,
first select SCIM under System Settings -> Keyboard -> Virtual Keyboard.

A compositor running on its own -- sway, river, labwc without a session manager
-- reads no XDG autostart entries, so nothing starts SCIM and section 1's
autostart entry is never used.  Start it from the compositor's own config
instead; in sway that is:

  exec scim -d

For X11 -- and for XWayland applications on a Wayland session -- set these
somewhere your session reads before it starts: ~/.xprofile, which the X session
startup scripts read on most distributions, or ~/.config/environment.d/*.conf,
which systemd reads.  Then log out and back in:

  GTK_IM_MODULE=scim
  QT_IM_MODULE=scim
  XMODIFIERS=@im=SCIM

The first two use SCIM's GTK and Qt IM modules.  XMODIFIERS covers everything
else through XIM, xterm and emacs included, but not GTK4: GTK4 dropped XIM, so a
GTK4 application on X11 needs the GTK4 IM module.

On KDE Plasma, the "Input Method Panel" applet -- kimpanel -- lets Plasma draw
the candidate window instead of SCIM, and SCIM uses it whenever it is there.  On
Wayland it may place that window at the top-left corner of the screen; leave the
applet off the panel, or set /Panel/UseKimpanel to false in
~/.config/scim/config, and SCIM draws the candidates itself.

GNOME on X11 can be an IBus session, but it does not have to be: 2.1 is one
option there, and setting these variables so SCIM is the input method itself is
the other.  Both work.


3. Using it
===========

On an IBus desktop (2.1), IBus and the desktop own the hotkeys.  Both default to
Super+space for the next input source, and GNOME adds Shift+Super+space for the
previous one.  Change them in GNOME's keyboard settings, or with ibus-setup.

On any other desktop (2.2), the hotkeys are SCIM's:

  Control+space       turn the input method on or off
  Control+Shift       switch engine -- hold both, then release Shift last for
                      the next engine, Control last for the previous one

Run scim-setup to change these, choose which engines are offered, and set the
candidate window's font and colours.

If nothing happens, restart the application first -- toolkits read their
input-method settings only at startup.


4. Packaging notes
==================

Regenerate the system IBus manifest from a package hook watching
$(libdir)/scim-1.0/$(SCIM_BINARY_VERSION)/IMEngine/, so it tracks which engines
are installed:

  scim -f ibus -- --xml > /usr/share/ibus/component/scim.xml

Run it as root, so it lists system-wide engines only; a user's own engines are
their business (2.1).  This matters only for IBus desktops.

Consider packaging the GTK and Qt IM modules separately from the core: GNOME
users want IBus's IM modules instead, other desktops want SCIM's.

SCIM ships an XDG autostart entry (scim.desktop) that runs `scim -d`, which
works on any desktop with XDG autostart support.  Keep it, or start the daemon
whichever way suits your platform -- a systemd user unit, your distribution's
own session mechanism, anything else -- as long as it comes up with the session.
That choice is yours.
